      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "{\u0022paragraphs\u0022:[[{\u0022sentence\u0022:\u0022The first risk is that supply constraints keep Keysight from turning strong demand into revenue, since management called the supply environment less flexible than a year ago and said supply chain will remain the governor of near-term revenue.\u0022,\u0022document\u0022:3},{\u0022sentence\u0022:\u0022That risk would be confirmed by fourth-quarter revenue landing below the low end of the $1.93 billion to $1.95 billion guidance range while orders stay above $2 billion.\u0022,\u0022document\u0022:1}],[{\u0022sentence\u0022:\u0022The second risk is that profitability normalises, because the chief financial officer cautioned that one-time tariff impacts artificially pulled up fiscal 2026 profitability and will not repeat, after an operating margin of 33.2% that exceeded the long-term target range of 31% to 32%.\u0022,\u0022document\u0022:3},{\u0022sentence\u0022:\u0022That risk would be confirmed by fourth-quarter non-GAAP earnings per share coming in below the low end of the $3.34 to $3.40 guidance range even though revenue reaches its guided range.\u0022,\u0022document\u0022:3}],[{\u0022sentence\u0022:\u0022The third risk is that orders lose momentum in the AI infrastructure segment that drove the quarter, where wireline revenue passed wireless for the first time and management said hyperscalers are roughly 10% of its business directly.\u0022,\u0022document\u0022:3},{\u0022sentence\u0022:\u0022That risk would be confirmed by fourth-quarter orders coming in below the $2.091 billion of the third quarter, when management\u0027s base case is orders slightly up from that level.\u0022,\u0022document\u0022:3}]]}"
